{
  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "prop:covariance-inflation-sharpness",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "In the binary one-stratum submodel with \\(\\mathcal H=\\{0\\}\\), \\(b=(1/2,1/2)\\), and scalar \\(V\\in(v_{\\min},v_{\\max})\\), let \\(q_{1-\\alpha}(V)\\) be the right \\((1-\\alpha)\\)-quantile of \\(S(0,Z_V)\\). There exist \\(c_1,c_2,s_0>0\\) such that \\(c_1s\\le q_{1-\\alpha}(V)-q_{1-\\alpha}(V-s)\\le c_2s\\) for \\(0<s<s_0\\). Hence a localized plug-in calibration that may underestimate \\(V\\) by \\(s\\) cannot be made uniformly honest with \\(o(s)\\) critical-value inflation.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "J",
      "K",
      "H",
      "b",
      "V",
      "vmin",
      "vmax",
      "alpha",
      "S",
      "ZV"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:gaussian-supremum",
      "lem:bregman-identity",
      "lem:path-wellposedness",
      "thm:atom-safe-envelope"
    ],
    "justification": "The result certifies the order of the covariance component when a user replaces the robust spectral envelope by localized plug-in calibration.",
    "gap": "CCK compares maxima under covariance perturbations, and Fang--Ridder--Xie links covariance to fixed-weight regret; neither gives the sharp quantile sensitivity of this nonlinear path supremum.",
    "consumer": "Implementers learn when the robust envelope can be localized and how much critical-value inflation a covariance undershoot necessarily costs.",
    "proof_tex": "In the stated binary submodel write \\(z\\) for the single contrast and\n\\[\n L(s)={e^s\\over1+e^s}.\n\\]\nFor \\(u=0\\), direct differentiation of the Bregman formula gives, for\n\\(z>0\\) and every \\(c>0\\),\n\\[\n {d\\over dz}\\ell_{c,b}(0,(0,z))\n ={z\\over c}L(z/c)\\{1-L(z/c)\\}>0.                                    \\tag{46}\n\\]\nThe uniform benchmark makes the loss even in \\(z\\), by interchanging the\ntwo action labels and using translation invariance of \\(\\Psi\\).  Its\nquadratic endpoint is \\(z^2/8\\), and the hard endpoint is zero.  Hence\n\\[\n s_0(r):=S(0,r)\n\\]\nis even and strictly increasing for \\(r>0\\).  To justify strictness for\nthe supremum, note that the quadratic endpoint is positive for \\(r>0\\),\nwhereas the finite-\\(c\\) expression tends to zero as \\(c\\downarrow0\\).\nThus, on any compact interval \\(0<r_-\\le r\\le r_+\\), every maximizing or\nasymptotically maximizing temperature lies in a compact subset of\n\\((0,\\infty]\\).  Equation (46), together with derivative \\(r/4\\) at\n\\(c=\\infty\\), then supplies constants \\(0<m<M<\\infty\\) such that\n\\[\n m(r_2-r_1)\\le s_0(r_2)-s_0(r_1)\\le M(r_2-r_1)                        \\tag{47}\n\\]\nwhenever \\(r_1<r_2\\) are sufficiently close in that compact interval.\n\nLet \\(a_\\alpha=\\Phi^{-1}(1-\\alpha/2)>0\\).  Since\n\\(|Z_V|=\\sqrt V\\,|Z_1|\\) and \\(s_0\\) is strictly increasing, its right\n\\((1-\\alpha)\\)-quantile is\n\\[\n q_{1-\\alpha}(V)=s_0(a_\\alpha\\sqrt V).                                \\tag{48}\n\\]\nFix the asserted interior \\(V\\).  For all sufficiently small \\(s>0\\),\nboth arguments in (48) lie in one compact interval away from zero, and\n\\[\n {a_\\alpha s\\over2\\sqrt V}\n \\le a_\\alpha\\{\\sqrt V-\\sqrt{V-s}\\}\n ={a_\\alpha s\\over\\sqrt V+\\sqrt{V-s}}\n \\le {a_\\alpha s\\over2\\sqrt{V-s}}.                                   \\tag{49}\n\\]\nCombining (47)--(49) gives constants \\(c_1,c_2,s_0>0\\) for which\n\\[\n c_1s\\le q_{1-\\alpha}(V)-q_{1-\\alpha}(V-s)\\le c_2s\n\\quad(0<s<s_0).\n\\]\nIf a localized calibration can replace \\(V\\) by \\(V-s\\), an inflation\nsmaller than the left side fails at the right quantile in (48).  The lower\nbound therefore rules out uniformly honest \\(o(s)\\) inflation, while the\nupper bound establishes order sharpness.",
